Let me implement the FB_Task.

Key logic:
- On invalid signal: alarm off, hold off, reset timer, no record, no runtime.
- valid & fault & not prev -> rise: increment counter, shift history, record timestamp.
- Alarm: active if valid AND (fault OR hold). Hold: timer TON of 500ms started when triggered. Actually simpler: use a TON timer that restarts on rising fault. Alarm active if valid AND (fault OR timer not elapsed).

Let me use TON timer: rtrig-ish. Use a hold timer: when fault rising edge (valid), start timer. Timer resets... Actually "Hold starts at elapsed zero on the trigger call and releases at 500ms if the fault is absent. A fresh rising trigger restarts the hold."

Simplest: maintain `holdTimer : TON`. On rising edge (valid & fault & !prev), call holdTimer(IN := FALSE) to reset then... Hmm TON with IN:=TRUE accumulates. We want hold timer running whenever there was a trigger. So set holdActive flag when rise, run TON, alarm = valid AND (fault OR NOT holdTimer.Q).

But TON.Q becomes true after 500ms. So hold = NOT holdTimer.Q while holdTimer running. If we start timer at trigger via IN:=TRUE, then after 500ms Q=TRUE, hold ends. Good.

Signal loss cancels hold: reset timer IN:=FALSE.

Let's structure:

```
// prevFaultTrack
prevValidFault : BOOL;
holdTimer : TON;
holdActive : BOOL;
```

Logic:
```
IF NOT THICKNESS_SIGNAL_VALID THEN
    THICKNESS_ALARM := FALSE;
    holdActive := FALSE;
    holdTimer(IN := FALSE);
    prevValidFault := FALSE;
ELSE
    rise := THICKNESS_FAULT AND NOT prevValidFault;
    IF rise THEN
        Slab_Fault_Counter := Slab_Fault_Counter + 1;
        // shift
        FOR i := 4 TO 1 BY -1 DO
            Slab_Fault_History[i] := Slab_Fault_History[i-1];
        END_FOR
        Slab_Fault_History[0] := FaultTimestamp;
        holdActive := TRUE;
        holdTimer(IN := FALSE);
    END_IF
    // run hold timer
    holdTimer(IN := holdActive);
    IF holdTimer.Q THEN
        holdActive := FALSE;
    END_IF
    THICKNESS_ALARM := THICKNESS_FAULT OR holdActive;
    // runtime
    IF CUTTING_RUN AND NOT THICKNESS_FAULT THEN
        Slab_Runtime_Accumulator := Slab_Runtime_Accumulator + TO_LTIME(CycleTime);
    END_IF
    prevValidFault := THICKNESS_FAULT;
END_IF
```

Wait the hold timer restart: on rise we set IN:=FALSE one scan which resets, then next line holdTimer(IN:=holdActive) with holdActive=TRUE. But CODESYS TON: calling with IN:=FALSE resets. Then calling again same scan with IN:=TRUE — that's two calls to same timer in a scan, allowed? Better to just reset properly. Actually calling timer twice in a scan is questionable. Let me restructure: use rising edge to reset timer by not calling FALSE separately.

Simpler: track elapsed hold time manually with LTIME accumulator? But scan semantics give dt_ms. Actually time base for timer - CODESYS TON uses system time, not dt_ms. In test harness they measure elapsed via actual time presumably. Hmm, scan_semantics says "dt_ms is elapsed time since preceding invocation; the first invocation starts IEC timers at elapsed zero." So real time advances by dt_ms between scans, TON should work if test harness sleeps? Likely they use a simulated timer... but standard TON relies on TIME(). In offline compile test, just need compile. Let's use TON.

For reset on rise: I can do:
```
IF rise THEN
    ...
    holdActive := TRUE;
    holdTimer(IN := FALSE);  // reset
END_IF
holdTimer(IN := holdActive AND NOT rise);  // hmm
```
Actually to restart at zero, reset then start. Two calls same scan: first resets, second starts. In CODESYS this generally works because each call updates internal state. But cleaner: use separate handling. Let me just do:

On rise: holdTimer(IN := FALSE); (resets ET to 0)
Then holdTimer(IN := TRUE) runs from 0.

But calling same TON instance twice per cycle is not clean. Alternative: maintain a boolean `holdActive`, and reset timer only when transitioning. Let me just call timer once per scan but reset by manipulating: actually standard pattern is to keep timer always called and use a separate "reset" via IN.

Let me think of TON semantics: TON(IN, PT) - when IN goes FALSE, ET resets to 0, Q false. When IN TRUE, ET accumulates until PT.

If I want to restart the hold on a fresh rise, I need ET reset. If IN stays TRUE across a rise, ET won't reset. But a rise requires prevValidFault false, meaning previous scan fault was false. If fault was false, was holdActive? During hold after fault clears, holdActive stays TRUE until 500ms. So a fresh rise while hold still active (fault went false then true within 500ms) should restart hold.

To restart, need to reset timer ET. Use: on rise, set a flag, and call timer with IN := FALSE this scan to reset, then holdActive running next... but then this scan alarm must still be active because fault present, so THICKNESS_ALARM = fault OR hold is TRUE anyway. So resetting timer this scan is fine; next scan it starts from 0 with holdActive.

So:
```
IF rise THEN
   record...
   holdActive := TRUE;
   holdTimer(IN := FALSE);   // reset ET
ELSE
   holdTimer(IN := holdActive);
   IF holdTimer.Q THEN holdActive := FALSE; END_IF
END_IF
THICKNESS_ALARM := THICKNESS_FAULT OR holdActive;
```
But if rise, we skip timer call this scan, timer ET=0, holdActive TRUE. Next scan (no rise) calls timer IN:=TRUE, accumulates. After 500ms Q true, holdActive false. Good. During rise scan, holdActive TRUE so alarm on. Good.

But there's subtlety: holdTimer.Q from previous... after reset Q false. Fine.

Edge: rise occurs, then invalid — handled by outer branch, resets everything.

For the record increment and history shift: FOR i := 4 TO 1 BY -1. Good.

Reset of prevValidFault when invalid: set FALSE. On returning valid with fault, rise = fault AND NOT prev = TRUE, so new occurrence. Matches "valid fault after signal loss is a new occurrence." Good.

Runtime: add CycleTime when valid AND NOT fault AND CUTTING_RUN. Test T3 step0: first invocation, CUTTING_RUN true, valid, no fault -> add 100ms. Good. Note CycleTime T#100ms -> TO_LTIME.

Power-up init: outputs initialized zero via declaration defaults. Need to init Slab_Fault_History array to zeros — default is zero anyway. But to be explicit maybe. Outputs declared with initial values.

Declaration:
```
FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task
VAR_INPUT
  THICKNESS_FAULT : BOOL;
  THICKNESS_SIGNAL_VALID : BOOL;
  CUTTING_RUN : BOOL;
  FaultTimestamp : DINT;
  CycleTime : TIME;
END_VAR
VAR_OUTPUT
  THICKNESS_ALARM : BOOL;
  Slab_Fault_Counter : UINT;
  Slab_Runtime_Accumulator : LTIME;
  Slab_Fault_History : ARRAY[0..4] OF DINT;
END_VAR
VAR
  prevValidFault : BOOL;
  holdActive : BOOL;
  holdTimer : TON;
  i : INT;
END_VAR
```

TO_LTIME(TIME) exists? In CODESYS, TIME to LTIME conversion: LTIME is long time, conversion function TO_LTIME maybe. Actually there is `TIME_TO_LTIME`? Let me use `TO_LTIME(CycleTime)`. Might need check. CODESYS supports implicit conversions? TYPE TIME is 32-bit ms, LTIME is 64-bit ns. Conversion functions: `LTIME_TO_TIME`, `TIME_TO_LTIME` exist in standard? I think `TO_LTIME` generic operator works. Let me use `TO_LTIME`.

Actually safer: Slab_Runtime_Accumulator := Slab_Runtime_Accumulator + TIME_TO_LTIME(CycleTime); Hmm. CODESYS 3.5 has conversion operators like `TIME_TO_LTIME`? There are standard conversion functions. I recall `TO_LTIME` works as type conversion. Let's try TO_LTIME first.

Let me write it.

Actually, note prevValidFault must be updated only... in valid branch we set at end. But on rise, prevValidFault updated at end = THICKNESS_FAULT = TRUE. Good.

Let me also consider the hold timer during rise scan: we don't call timer so ET stays from reset. Fine.

But there's an issue: on the scan after rise, holdActive TRUE, timer IN TRUE, but fault may still be TRUE (persistent). Alarm on due to fault. Timer Q after 500ms -> holdActive false but fault keeps alarm on. When fault clears, no hold, alarm off immediately? But requirement: hold for at least 500ms per trigger. If fault persists >500ms then clears, should there still be hold? "hold each triggered alarm for at least 500 ms. Keep the alarm active while the fault persists." The hold is from trigger time. If fault persisted longer than 500ms, hold already elapsed, so on clear alarm turns off. That matches atomic "hold: trigger age<500ms with no intervening signal loss". Good.

In T1: step1 trigger at dt... step2 fault false, dt100 -> hold active (100<500) alarm true. step3 dt300 -> total 400, alarm true. step4 dt100 -> total 500, alarm false. Let's verify timer accumulation: trigger scan step1 (rise) resets timer ET=0, holdActive true. step2: dt_ms 100 but timer uses real time. Hmm timer ET accumulates real elapsed 100ms -> 100. step3 +300=400. step4 +100=500 -> Q true -> holdActive false, alarm = fault(false) OR hold(false)=false. Good, exactly 500 releases.

But note: TON releases when ET >= PT. At exactly 500 it's >= so Q true. Good.

